"An earlier lesson in this unit described fear as driving qi sharply downward. Anger is traditionally described as producing the opposite directional effect: \"anger makes qi rise,\" sending qi surging sharply upward, away from its normal, evenly distributed circulation, in the same way fear sends it sharply down.": "0600d2546e16",
"An earlier lesson in this unit described fear as driving qi sharply downward. Anger is traditionally described as producing the opposite directional effect: \"anger makes qi rise,\" sending qi surging sharply upward, away from its normal, evenly distributed circulation, in the same way fear sends it sharply down. This upward surge is offered as the traditional explanation for a cluster of symptoms familiar to almost anyone who has been genuinely furious: a flushed, reddened face, a headache concentrated at the temples or the top of the head, and in more intense cases, a spell of dizziness or light-headedness as qi rushes upward faster than the body can accommodate it. The liver, covered extensively elsewhere in this unit as the organ most closely tied to mood and blood storage, is traditionally identified as the organ most directly affected by this upward surge, since anger is its associated emotion. Reading anger through this framework also explains why traditional advice for anger has so often centered on physically calming or grounding actions — slow breathing, sitting down, stepping away — rather than only mental techniques aimed at the emotion itself: if anger's primary physical effect is a qi surge moving upward, actions that encourage qi to settle back downward address the same mechanism directly, working with the body's own qi mechanism rather than only trying to talk the emotion down.": "15a09b98a740",
